      "text": "A smaller size doesn't change the math, and it doesn't change my mandate. Let me be precise about what you're now asking.\n\nYou're asking me to deploy capital into a wallet whose verified 30-day realized PnL is **−4,745,429 USD**, on the theory that a 20% position is \"cheap information.\" But the mandate is explicit: I must mirror exactly one wallet, no splitting, no partial allocation. A 20% position is not one of my two options — it's a blend, and abstaining or blending isn't available. If I choose A, I choose A at the slot. And the information you're buying is already in hand: this is a wallet that lost five million dollars in a month on two positions (SOL −4,472,138, XRP −630,773) while manufacturing a flattering win rate out of thousands of sub-dollar reduce fills.\n\nThe \"fresh data in days\" argument actually betrays the case. I already pulled the freshest data: the trailing 7 days show a **1.000 win rate across 424 trades** on 3 instruments, with a gold position whose fee load (1,223 USD) eats nearly a fifth of its own gain (7,674 USD) and a PUMP line contributing exactly 0 PnL on 253 USD of fees. That is not a live edge revealing itself — that's the residue of a blown-up book still generating cosmetic fills. Adding our own reporting layer won't turn a −20.5% month into a process.\n\nWeigh the two mandates side by side:\n- **Wallet A:** −4.75M USD realized / 30d. Breadth and win-rate metrics shown to be artifacts of duplicate reduce-fill accounting. Recent window is a degenerate 100%-win micro-scalp book.\n- **Wallet B:** +35,083 USD realized / 30d, 60.3% win rate over 2,064 trades, 1,404 USD fees, −1,208 USD over the last week — a normal drawdown on a nominally positive book.\n\nThe only metric that survives scrutiny — realized PnL — is catastrophically negative for A and positive for B. I won't mirror a wallet that is down five million dollars on the strength of a win-rate statistic I've verified is an accounting artifact, at any size, since partial sizing isn't available to me.\n\nCHOICE: B",
